#!/bin/sh
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 DGR Operations <repo@dagric.com>
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# Dagric OS — call the apps what they ARE.
#
# A Windows switcher opens the launcher and reads: Dolphin, Konsole, Gwenview,
# Okular, Ark, Elisa, Spectacle, Kate. Not one of those words tells them what
# the program does. This regenerates a renamed copy of each .desktop file so
# the menu says Files, Terminal, Photos, Document Viewer, Archive Manager.
#
# WHY /usr/local/share/applications AND NOT AN EDIT IN PLACE
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# Editing /usr/share/applications/*.desktop is simpler, and it is what
# 0500-desktop-defaults does to the Calamares entry. That is fine THERE: the
# installer entry only has to survive the live session. It is wrong here.
# Dagric is installed once and then updated for years, and dpkg restores the
# packaged file on every upgrade of dolphin/konsole/okular/... — so an in-place
# edit means "Files" silently turns back into "Dolphin" the first time KDE gets
# a point release, on a machine whose owner has no idea why or how to fix it.
# A rename that expires is worse than no rename.
#
# So we write a COMPLETE copy into /usr/local/share/applications instead.
# Verified on the target, not assumed:
#   * nothing in the base system sets XDG_DATA_DIRS (/etc/profile, /etc/profile.d,
#     /etc/environment and the systemd user-environment generators are all clean),
#     and startplasma-x11/startplasma-wayland reference XDG_CONFIG_DIRS only —
#     never XDG_DATA_DIRS. So the spec default applies:
#         ~/.local/share : /usr/local/share : /usr/share
#   * for one desktop-file-id the FIRST directory wins, whole file. There is no
#     key-level merge, which is why the copy has to be complete rather than a
#     stub carrying just Name=.
# dpkg never writes to /usr/local, so the rename cannot be reverted by an upgrade.
#
# The price of shadowing a whole file is that the copy freezes Exec=, MimeType=
# and the Desktop Actions as they were the day it was written — so an upgrade
# that adds a MIME type or changes a command line would be masked by our stale
# copy. That is why this is a REGENERATOR and not a set of hand-written files,
# and why /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/99dagric-app-names re-runs it after every package
# operation: the override is re-derived from whatever upstream currently ships,
# and only the naming keys are ours. Build time writes it once; apt keeps it true.
#
# KEEPING THE ORIGINAL NAME FINDABLE — the part that must not regress
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Someone reading a forum post that says "open Dolphin" has to be able to find
# it. Verified from the shipped Trixie binary rather than assumed: the KRunner
# services plugin
#     /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/qt6/plugins/kf6/krunner/krunner_services.so
# imports KService::keywords() (alongside name(), genericName() and
# untranslatedName()), so Keywords= really is searched — and Kickoff, KRunner
# and the Overview effect all sit on that same Plasma Search stack, so putting
# the old name in Keywords= covers all three. Every rename below therefore keeps
# the upstream name as a keyword, and repeats it in GenericName, which Kickoff
# renders as the second line under the app name (appNameFormat defaults to
# NameAndGenericName). The menu reads "Files / File manager (Dolphin)", so the
# window title bar — which still says Dolphin, and which no .desktop file can
# change — agrees with the menu instead of contradicting it.
#
# THE en_GB LANDMINE
# ------------------
# KDE ships Name[en_GB] on nearly every file, and Debian's gdebi also ships
# Name[en_AU] and Name[en_CA]. Setting Name= alone leaves everyone who picked
# English (UK/AU/CA) in the installer looking at "Dolphin" — a large slice of
# exactly this audience. The rewriter drops every Name[en*]/GenericName[en*]/
# Keywords[en*] line so our untranslated value is what they get.
#
# THE OTHER LANGUAGES — answered, and here is how
# -----------------------------------------------
# This block used to say only English locale lines were dropped, and that a
# German install therefore kept Name[de]=Dolphin: the rename simply did not
# reach non-English installs. That was the right call while Dagric's own UI was
# English-only. It is not any more. A French owner running a French Hub, a
# French setup wizard and a French migration assistant, whose launcher still
# says "Dolphin" and "Gwenview", gets the worst of both — the rename is the one
# feature of this product a switcher notices in the first thirty seconds, and
# it was the only part still speaking English at them.
#
# So the names below now carry translations, and three things follow from that.
#
# (a) THE TRANSLATIONS CANNOT COME FROM gettext. This script runs as root from
#     an apt hook, in whatever locale root happens to have, and the file it
#     writes is read later by EVERY user on the machine. "The current language"
#     is meaningless here. A .desktop file has to carry every language at once,
#     so the catalogue is static data — applang_data() below — and rename()
#     emits Name[de], Name[fr], Name[es], Name[pt_BR] and Name[it] side by side.
#
# (b) EMITTING Name[de] MEANS DROPPING UPSTREAM'S Name[de]. Two Name[de] lines
#     in one [Desktop Entry] group is undefined by the spec, and implementations
#     genuinely disagree: GLib's key-file parser and KConfig do not resolve a
#     duplicate the same way, so you would get "Dateien" in Kickoff and
#     "Dolphin" in a GTK app's Open With dialog. Strip-then-emit, per language,
#     is mandatory — and it is done per EXACT postfix, so emitting Name[pt_BR]
#     removes only Name[pt_BR] and leaves upstream's Name[pt] alone for a
#     Portuguese owner in Portugal.
#
# (c) KEYWORDS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED. Keywords= is a search index, not
#     prose, and it is the one field machine translation reliably ruins:
#     "dolphin;explorer;this pc;" comes back with the fish and a literal
#     "dieser PC", losing the actual German Windows words a switcher would type.
#     Upstream's own Keywords[de] is written by KDE's German team and is good;
#     leaving it in place costs nothing, creates no duplicate key, and keeps the
#     English Keywords= as the fallback for every other language. When a native
#     speaker writes a proper Keywords[xx] for these, it goes in applang_data()
#     as a fourth column and the awk gains one more strip pattern.
#
# The English Name[en*]/GenericName[en*]/Keywords[en*] strip is unchanged and
# still unconditional — see THE en_GB LANDMINE above.
set -e

SRC_DIR=/usr/share/applications
DST_DIR=/usr/local/share/applications

# Marker written into every file we generate. Nothing is ever deleted unless it
# carries this line, so a file an administrator dropped in by hand is safe.
MARK='# Generated by dagric-app-names — edits here are overwritten on upgrade.'

mkdir -p "$DST_DIR"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The edition gate, enforced on every apt run and not only at build time.
#
# 0510-app-names.hook.chroot deletes the X-Dagric-Edition=pro launcher entries
# from a free image while it is being built, and that was enough for as long as
# nothing could ever put them back. The update channel changes that: the four
# dagric-* packages carry one file list for both editions, so an `apt upgrade`
# of dagric-tools on a FREE machine unpacks dagric-usb-protect.desktop and
# dagric-vm.desktop straight back into the menu — and a free owner would then
# find two entries for tools their edition does not have. That is the "free
# edition reads as BROKEN" failure, arriving through the one channel designed to
# fix things.
#
# It is two, not three. dagric-gaming.desktop used to carry the flag as well and
# no longer does: the helper runs on both editions, its Hub row is offered on
# both, and the free manual page tells its owner to open it from the launcher —
# so deleting that entry from free was itself the failure this gate exists to
# prevent. Do not add it back; see the comment in the .desktop file.
#
# This script is already what /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/99dagric-app-names runs after
# every successful dpkg operation, which makes it the one place the gate can be
# applied at exactly the moment it is needed. Build time keeps its own delete;
# this is what keeps it true for the years afterwards.
#
# Read from /etc/dagric-edition, the same file the hooks read, and default to
# free — an unreadable edition file must never hand out the Pro menu.
EDITION=$(cat /etc/dagric-edition 2>/dev/null || echo free)
if [ "$EDITION" != "pro" ]; then
    for _pro in $(grep -rlx 'X-Dagric-Edition=pro' "$SRC_DIR" 2>/dev/null); do
        rm -f "$_pro" "$DST_DIR/${_pro##*/}"
    done

    # The Pro LAYOUTS and STYLES arrive by the same route and were not covered.
    # build.sh (and docker/container-build.sh) delete every EDITION=pro drop-in
    # under share/dagric/looks and share/dagric/styles from a free image, and
    # state exactly why: "otherwise a free user could unlock the Pro layouts and
    # styles by deleting one line of text". dagric-appearance's own gate is that
    # one line — it reads the EDITION key and skips the entry — so the files
    # must not be present at all.
    #
    # But dagric-tools carries ONE file list for both editions and packs the
    # whole looks/ and styles/ trees (packages/stage-packages.sh, the
    # "firstrun appearance manual guide welcome styles looks hwcheck boot"
    # loop), so the first `apt upgrade` on a free machine unpacks all four Pro
    # layouts, all three Pro styles and their gallery thumbs straight back —
    # leaving nothing between a free owner and the Pro appearance set except the
    # single flag the build scripts refused to rely on. Same failure as the
    # launcher entries above, same channel, so: same delete, same moment.
    #
    # The thumbnail goes with the drop-in for the reason build.sh gives — an
    # orphan thumb is dead weight, and a gallery listing it would advertise a
    # style this edition can no longer apply. Only /usr/share is touched;
    # /etc/dagric and the owner's ~/.local/share drop-ins are theirs.
    for _pro in $(grep -rlx 'EDITION=pro' /usr/share/dagric/looks \
        /usr/share/dagric/styles 2>/dev/null); do
        _base=${_pro##*/}
        rm -f "$_pro" "/usr/share/dagric/appearance/thumbs/${_base%.*}.png"
    done
    unset _base
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The translated names.
#
# Columns, TAB separated:   language   desktop-id   Name   GenericName
#
# MACHINE TRANSLATION, NOT REVIEWED BY A NATIVE SPEAKER. Same status as
# po/*.po; see the header there for what has to be checked before a language is
# advertised. These follow the same rules as the English names above: name the
# job not the product, never a competitor's exact product name, no "KDE" in
# Name=, and NO "&" anywhere in a Name — Qt reads it as a menu accelerator and
# eats the following letter.
#
# A language with no row for an id simply gets no Name[xx] for it, and the
# desktop falls back to the unlocalised Name= per the spec. That is a clean,
# defined outcome, so a half-filled table is safe.
applang_data() {
    cat << 'APPLANG'
